## Features

| | |
| --- | --- |
| **Storage** | External PostgreSQL (default) or local RocksDB as the database; S3 or any S3-compatible service for mail blobs |
| **Declarative settings** | Blob store, server hostname and default domain, Prometheus exporter, logging, OIDC directory, in-memory store, cluster coordinator, encryption at rest, blob encryption and compression, Enterprise license and logo applied by a CLI Job; anything else through `provisioning.extraPlan` |
| **System accounts** | Send-only accounts for other applications, with app passwords published to Secrets |
| **Exposure** | Mail Service for SMTP, IMAP, POP3 and ManageSieve (LoadBalancer or NodePort); Ingress or Gateway API HTTPRoute for the web UI, JMAP and DAV; Gateway API TCPRoutes for mail ports; dual-stack |
| **Scaling** | HorizontalPodAutoscaler with a conservative scale-down, PodDisruptionBudget, anti-affinity presets, topology spread, NATS/Redis/Kafka/Zenoh coordination |
| **Security** | Non-root, read-only root filesystem, all capabilities dropped except `NET_BIND_SERVICE`, no ServiceAccount token in the Stalwart and webmail pods, images pinned by digest, optional NetworkPolicies and Kyverno policies |
| **Observability** | Prometheus exporter with optional Basic authentication and ServiceMonitor; logs to stdout |
| **Certificates** | Mounted certificates (for example from cert-manager) reloaded on a schedule without restarting pods |
| **Webmail** | Bulwark JMAP webmail with OAuth/OIDC login, branding (logos, colours, login page), its own autoscaling, PDB and persistence |
| **Supply chain** | Chart published to GHCR as an OCI artifact and signed with cosign; Renovate keeps images, actions and tools current |

## Requirements

- Kubernetes 1.27 or later, and Helm 3.8 or later (Helm 4 is used in CI).
- PostgreSQL for the default database, reachable from the cluster. RocksDB needs a persistent volume and limits you to one replica.
- An S3 bucket, unless you set `blobStore.backend: existing` and configure blob storage yourself.
- For more than one Stalwart replica, a shared external database, a shared Redis-compatible in-memory store (Redis or Valkey) and a cluster coordinator (NATS, Redis, Kafka or Zenoh) are required.
- Stalwart 1.0 cannot run on a 0.16 database: chart versions before 0.2.0 deployed Stalwart 0.16, and their data must be migrated with Stalwart's [migration tool](https://stalw.art/docs/migration/upgrade-0-16/) or the installation started fresh.
- Optional: Prometheus Operator CRDs for the ServiceMonitor; Gateway API CRDs for routes; cert-manager for certificates.

## Install

Create the namespace and the Secrets the chart refers to. The names below match [examples/postgresql-s3.yaml](examples/postgresql-s3.yaml):

| Secret | Keys |
| --- | --- |
| `stalwart-postgresql` | `password`: database password |
| `stalwart-s3` | `accessKeyId`, `secretKey`: bucket credentials |
| `stalwart-admin` | `password`: administrator password for the provisioning Job; `STALWART_RECOVERY_ADMIN`: `admin:<same password>` for the first start |

```sh
kubectl create namespace stalwart
kubectl --namespace stalwart create secret generic stalwart-postgresql --from-literal=password='<db-password>'
kubectl --namespace stalwart create secret generic stalwart-s3 \
  --from-literal=accessKeyId='<access-key-id>' --from-literal=secretKey='<secret-key>'
kubectl --namespace stalwart create secret generic stalwart-admin \
  --from-literal=password='<admin-password>' --from-literal=STALWART_RECOVERY_ADMIN='admin:<admin-password>'
```

Copy the example to `my-values.yaml`, set the database host, bucket, `hostname` and `domain`, and install from the OCI registry:

```sh
helm upgrade --install stalwart oci://ghcr.io/s7n-systems/charts/stalwart \
  --version {{ template "chart.version" . }} --namespace stalwart -f my-values.yaml --timeout 15m
```

The chart validates required storage settings and credential references before rendering. It cannot check whether referenced Secrets exist or whether external services are reachable; these failures can still leave an incomplete installation. To install from a checkout instead, use `.` as the chart: Bitnami Common is vendored in `charts/`, so no dependency download is needed.

### Verify the signature

Release builds are signed keylessly with cosign 3 from this repository's release workflow. Cosign 3 stores the signature as an OCI referrer in the new bundle format; verify with cosign 3 or newer (cosign 2 reports "no signatures found"):

```sh
cosign verify ghcr.io/s7n-systems/charts/stalwart:{{ template "chart.version" . }} \
  --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github\.com/s7n-systems/stalwart-chart/\.github/workflows/release\.yml@' \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com
```

## First-time setup

For an interactive first-time setup, use recovery mode, which suspends mail services and opens only the management listener. You can instead provision domains and other settings through `provisioning.extraPlan` during a normal installation.

```sh
helm upgrade --install stalwart oci://ghcr.io/s7n-systems/charts/stalwart --version {{ template "chart.version" . }} \
  --namespace stalwart -f my-values.yaml --set recoveryMode.enabled=true --timeout 15m
kubectl --namespace stalwart port-forward svc/stalwart-management 8080:8080
```

Open <http://localhost:8080/admin>, sign in with the recovery administrator, and create domains, accounts and anything else you need. Then run the same `helm upgrade` with `recoveryMode.enabled=false`.

Afterwards, point the provisioning credential at a permanent administrator. If `recoveryAdmin.enabled` is set, disable it before removing its referenced Secret key. Remove `STALWART_RECOVERY_ADMIN` from any Secret loaded through `existingSecret`, then restart the pods. The provisioning Job needs a valid administrator on every upgrade.


## Configuration

Every option is listed under [Values](#values). This section explains how the parts fit together.

### Images

The Stalwart, CLI and Bulwark images are pinned by digest. **Changing a tag alone does not change the image:** set the matching `digest` too, or clear it to follow the tag. This applies to `image`, `provisioning.image` and `webmail.image`. With digests, `pullPolicy: IfNotPresent` is always correct. `global.imageRegistry` replaces all three registries for a mirror.

[Renovate](renovate.json) opens weekly PRs that move tag and digest together; for Stalwart it also moves `appVersion`. Keep image blocks in `values.yaml` in the order `registry`, `repository`, `tag`, `digest`, or Renovate stops matching them.

### Credentials

Prefer existing Secrets: plain values are stored in Helm's release history, even though the chart puts them in a Kubernetes Secret.

| Credential | Existing Secret settings |
| --- | --- |
| Recovery administrator | `recoveryAdmin.existingSecret`, `.secretKey` (value `username:password`) |
| PostgreSQL | `dataStore.postgresql.existingSecret`, `.passwordSecretKey` |
| S3 | `blobStore.s3.existingSecret`, `.accessKeyIdSecretKey`, `.secretKeySecretKey` |
| Provisioning administrator | `provisioning.existingSecret`, `.passwordSecretKey` (password only; `provisioning.username` is separate) |
| Metrics | `metrics.auth.existingSecret`, `.passwordSecretKey` |
| NATS | `coordinator.nats.existingSecret`, `.passwordSecretKey` |
| Bulwark | `webmail.sessionSecret`, `webmail.adminPassword`, `webmail.oauth` |
| Image pulls | `global.imagePullSecrets` or each image's `pullSecrets` |

The top-level `existingSecret` is loaded into the server's environment with `envFrom`. For single variables use `extraEnvVars` (full `env` entries, including `secretKeyRef`); `extraEnv` takes plain values, and `extraSecretEnv` stores values in the chart's own Secret. Pods read environment variables at start-up, so restart them after changing a Secret.

### Storage

`dataStore.backend` selects `postgresql` (default) or `rocksdb`. A non-empty `config` object replaces this with a raw startup configuration for other backends. RocksDB and SQLite data stores need persistent storage and cannot run with more than one replica. The chart defaults to `persistence.enabled: false`; enable it for a local database. Custom local paths must match the mounted data volume (`/var/lib/stalwart`) or use additional volumes.

`blobStore.backend: s3` stores messages in a bucket. Use Stalwart's region names such as `EuCentral1`; for MinIO, SeaweedFS or another S3-compatible service set `custom.url` and `custom.region`. Only static access keys are supported. `blobStore.backend: existing` leaves blob storage as configured elsewhere.

Changing the blob store does not migrate existing mail.

### Server settings

The chart applies these settings with a `stalwart-cli apply` Job after every install and upgrade:

| Values | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `blobStore.backend: s3` | Configures the S3 blob store |
| `hostname` | Sets the server hostname and default domain (`mailDomain`, default `domain`) |
| `logging.console.enabled` (default) | Logs to stdout and disables the default log file |
| `metrics.enabled`, `metrics.provision` | Enables the Prometheus exporter |
| `encryption.provision` | Applies the server-wide encryption-at-rest switch (`encryption.atRestEnable`) |
| `encryption.blobKey.existingSecret`, `blobStore.compression` | Creates the blob encryption key and sets blob compression, see [Blob encryption and compression](#blob-encryption-and-compression) |
| `oidc.enabled` | Declares an OIDC directory; `oidc.activate` switches authentication to it |
| `systemAccounts.accounts` | Creates system accounts; the reconcile Job writes their app passwords, see [System accounts](#system-accounts) |
| `inMemoryStore.type` | Configures the shared in-memory store |
| `coordinator.type` | Configures cluster coordination |
| `provisioning.extraPlan` | Applies further operations after the chart's own |

Set `hostname` on every installation, usually to the public mail host. A new server has none, so JMAP session URLs would point at the pod name. Stalwart requires a default domain with it: the Job creates `mailDomain` (default: `domain`) if it is missing, matched by name, and makes it the default.

`provisioning.extraPlan` takes any `stalwart-cli apply` operation, one object per entry; it runs after the chart's own entries, so it can refer to them and override them. For example, an additional domain:

```yaml
hostname: mail.example.org
domain: example.org
provisioning:
  extraPlan:
    - {"@type": upsert, object: Domain, matchOn: [name], value: {second: {name: example.net}}}
```

Stalwart 1.0 renamed and restructured many settings; plans written for 0.16 fail with errors such as `invalidPatch`. See Stalwart's [schema changes](https://github.com/stalwartlabs/stalwart-migrate/blob/main/SCHEMA_CHANGES.md).

After provisioning, the reconciliation Job restarts Stalwart when server settings change and waits for readiness. Interrupted runs resume the pending rollout. A database reset triggers another restart. This requires `updateStrategy: RollingUpdate` without a partition.

The Job uses a restart instead of `ReloadSettings`, so installation does not require a reachable OIDC provider. See [reconciliation](docs/reconciliation.md) for restart markers and Secret ownership.

The Job needs an administrator Stalwart already accepts. It talks to the internal management Service over HTTP; successful Jobs are deleted, failed ones stay for inspection:

```sh
kubectl --namespace stalwart logs job/stalwart-provisioning
```

Use a Helm timeout longer than `provisioning.activeDeadlineSeconds`. Rollback and uninstall do not undo settings in Stalwart's database, and turning an option off does not revert what it applied.

### Networking

The chart creates the management and headless Services, plus the mail Service when `service.enabled: true` (the default). Enabling webmail adds its own Service:

| Service | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `<fullname>` | Mail ports from `service.ports`. Set `service.type: LoadBalancer` (or `NodePort`) to reach them from outside. |
| `<fullname>-management` | Stalwart's plain HTTP listener (port 8080): web UI, JMAP, DAV, metrics. Always `ClusterIP`. |
| `<fullname>-headless` | StatefulSet pod identity. |

`service.ports` defaults to what mail clients need: SMTP on 25, implicit-TLS submission on 465, IMAPS on 993 and HTTPS on 443; management uses plain HTTP on 8080. SMTP on 25 starts in plaintext and can negotiate STARTTLS. Stalwart also listens on 995 (POP3S) and 4190 (ManageSieve), which you can add as ports. With an Ingress or Gateway in front, drop `https` (`https: null`), so the mail Service carries mail ports only.

A Kubernetes port does not make Stalwart listen. To offer a STARTTLS port such as submission on 587 (for clients that cannot use 465), add the listener in Stalwart, then the port:

```yaml
service:
  type: LoadBalancer
  ports:
    submission: {port: 587, containerPort: 587, protocol: TCP}
provisioning:
  extraPlan:
    - {"@type": upsert, object: NetworkListener, matchOn: [name],
       value: {submission: {name: submission, protocol: smtp, bind: {"[::]:587": true}}}}
```

`values.yaml` has commented entries for 995 (POP3S), 4190 (ManageSieve), 587 (submission), 143 (IMAP), 110 (POP3) and 80 (HTTP).

`externalTrafficPolicy: Local` (the default for external Services) keeps client IP addresses, which SMTP needs for SPF, rate limits and block lists. `service.ipFamilyPolicy` and `service.ipFamilies` enable dual-stack.

#### Ingress

The Ingress carries HTTP only; SMTP, IMAP, POP3 and ManageSieve need the mail Service or TCPRoutes. By default (`ingress.backend: management`) it forwards to the management Service, with TLS ending at the controller:

```yaml
ingress:
  enabled: true
  className: nginx
  hosts:
    - host: mail.example.org
      paths: [{path: /, pathType: Prefix}]
  tls:
    - secretName: mail-example-org-tls
      hosts: [mail.example.org]
```

With top-level `domain` set, an empty `ingress.hosts` defaults to `mail.<domain>`. Behind a proxy, Stalwart sees the proxy's address; let it take the client address from `X-Forwarded-For` when only the Ingress and the cluster reach the HTTP listener (`forwardedHops`: the number of trusted proxies in front of Stalwart):

```yaml
provisioning:
  extraPlan:
    - {"@type": update, object: Http, value: {clientIpSource: xForwardedFor, forwardedHops: 1}}
```

`ingress.backend: mail` forwards to the mail Service's `https` port instead, Stalwart's TLS listener; your controller then needs its HTTPS-upstream setting.

#### Gateway API

Routes attach to existing Gateways. The HTTPRoute also defaults to the management Service:

```yaml
gatewayAPI:
  httpRoute:
    enabled: true
    parentRefs:
      - {name: web-gateway, namespace: infrastructure, sectionName: https}
    hostnames: [mail.example.org]
  tcpRoutes:
    smtp:
      parentRefs:
        - {name: mail-gateway, namespace: infrastructure, sectionName: smtp}
```

Each `tcpRoutes` key names a `service.ports` entry and needs a TCP listener on the Gateway; the mail protocol's TLS passes through unchanged. Set `gatewayAPI.tcpRouteApiVersion` to `gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1alpha2` for older CRDs. Cross-namespace Gateways must allow routes from the release namespace.

### TLS certificates

Stalwart can obtain certificates itself over ACME. To use certificates managed by cert-manager instead, mount the Secret, register it in Stalwart, and enable periodic certificate reloads:

```yaml
extraVolumes:
  - name: tls
    secret:
      secretName: mail-example-org-tls
extraVolumeMounts:
  - name: tls
    mountPath: /etc/stalwart/tls
    readOnly: true

provisioning:
  extraPlan:
    - "@type": upsert
      object: Certificate
      matchOn: [certificate]
      value:
        main-tls:
          certificate: {"@type": File, filePath: /etc/stalwart/tls/tls.crt}
          privateKey: {"@type": File, filePath: /etc/stalwart/tls/tls.key}
    - {"@type": update, object: SystemSettings, value: {defaultCertificateId: "#main-tls"}}

certificateReload:
  enabled: true
```

Stalwart reads certificate files only at start-up or on `ReloadTlsCertificates`. `certificateReload` runs that action from a CronJob (daily by default, `certificateReload.schedule`) with the provisioning credentials, so renewed certificates are picked up at the next successful scheduled reload without a restart. It does not watch Secret changes.

### Monitoring

`metrics.enabled: true` with `metrics.provision: true` (the default) configures Stalwart's Prometheus exporter at `/metrics/prometheus` on the management Service. Set `metrics.auth` for Basic authentication: a cluster-internal Service alone does not restrict who can read it. `metrics.provision: false` leaves an exporter configured elsewhere untouched.

`metrics.serviceMonitor.enabled: true` adds a ServiceMonitor (Prometheus Operator CRDs required). It always lives in the release namespace: configure Prometheus's `serviceMonitorNamespaceSelector` and `serviceMonitorSelector` to find it.

### Authentication

`oidc.enabled` provisions an OIDC directory. Set `oidc.issuerUrl` to the provider's issuer URL and use `oidc.claimUsername` to match accounts. `oidc.activate: true` makes this the server-wide directory for every protocol.

Stalwart validates bearer tokens without a client secret. The token audience must match a Stalwart resource identifier or `oidc.acceptedAudiences`.

`oidc.unscopedTokens` controls tokens without Stalwart scopes (`mail`, `contacts`, `calendars`, `files`):

| Value | Access |
| --- | --- |
| `reject` | Reject the token (default) |
| `interoperable` | Allow those four scopes, without management access |
| `full` | Full access |

External-directory accounts use app passwords for IMAP and SMTP; administrators cannot set their passwords. Accounts can be created on first OIDC login. Pre-create accounts that must receive mail before then. The chart does not provision ordinary users. Test administrator and mail-client logins before activation; the recovery administrator remains available.

`encryption.provision: true` applies the server-wide `encryption.atRestEnable` switch (enabled by default in Stalwart). Each account must also enable encryption with a registered OpenPGP key or S/MIME certificate and choose whether to encrypt client-uploaded messages. This does not enable S3 bucket encryption; for that, see the next section.

### Blob encryption and compression

Stalwart can encrypt the blobs it stores (message bodies and attachments, and WebDAV files) with a key held by the server, so the blob store, such as an S3 provider, holds only ciphertext. Unlike encryption at rest, Stalwart can still read, index and search the messages. Headers, previews and the search index in the database are not covered.

Create the key as 32 random bytes, base64-encoded, and keep a copy outside the cluster: blobs written with it cannot be read without it.

```sh
kubectl create secret generic stalwart-blob-key --from-literal=key="$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
```

```yaml
encryption:
  blobKey:
    existingSecret: stalwart-blob-key
blobStore:
  compression: zstd   # optional: lz4 (Stalwart's default), zstd or none
```

The provisioning Job creates a `SymmetricKey` that reads the key from the `STALWART_BLOB_KEY` environment variable, so the key never appears in the plan. The reconcile Job then makes it the key for new mail and file blobs and restarts Stalwart. `encryption.blobKey.email: false` or `fileStorage: false` stops encrypting new blobs of that kind; blobs already encrypted stay readable as long as the key exists. Stalwart records the key's fingerprint: if the key in the Secret or `cipher` changes later, Stalwart refuses to load the key and cannot store new mail until the original key is back.

Existing blobs keep their previous encryption and compression. To rewrite them, look up the key's id and start a `BlobRecode` task once through the management Service (`kubectl port-forward svc/<fullname>-management 8080:8080`):

```sh
curl -u admin:PASSWORD -H 'Content-Type: application/json' http://localhost:8080/jmap/ -d '{
  "using": ["urn:ietf:params:jmap:core", "urn:stalwart:jmap"],
  "methodCalls": [["x:SymmetricKey/get", {"properties": ["description"]}, "0"]]
}'
curl -u admin:PASSWORD -H 'Content-Type: application/json' http://localhost:8080/jmap/ -d '{
  "using": ["urn:ietf:params:jmap:core", "urn:stalwart:jmap"],
  "methodCalls": [["x:Task/set", {"create": {"recode": {"@type": "BlobRecode",
    "compression": "keep", "encryption": {"@type": "Encrypt", "keyId": "KEY_ID"}}}}, "0"]]
}'
```

`compression` takes `keep`, `disable`, `lz4` or `zstd`. Stalwart logs its progress as `store.blob-store-recoded`. Plaintext copies written before can survive the rewrite: in RocksDB until compaction, and in S3 object versions and backups.

### Enterprise edition

The settings under `enterprise` need a paid **Enterprise license** from [Stalwart Labs](https://stalw.art/pricing/) (sponsors on OpenCollective get one too). Without a valid license, Stalwart logs a configuration warning and runs as the community edition: it ignores them and keeps working. They require provisioning credentials.

The license can come from a Secret instead of the WebUI. A license is issued for the server's hostname (`hostname`) and a number of accounts. With an API key, Stalwart downloads the license and renews it before it expires; with a license key, replace the Secret before the license expires:

```yaml
enterprise:
  apiKey:
    existingSecret: stalwart-enterprise   # key api-key
  # or a fixed license:
  # licenseKey:
  #   existingSecret: stalwart-license    # key license-key
  logo: "data:image/svg+xml;base64,PHN2Zy..."   # base64 -w0 logo.svg
```

Set one of the two: Stalwart stores the license it renews in place of the configured key, which the chart would overwrite on every upgrade. Stalwart reads the key only at startup, and the chart cannot see changes to your Secret, so restart Stalwart after replacing it:

```sh
kubectl -n <namespace> rollout restart statefulset/<fullname>
```
 `enterprise.logo` is the default logo of Stalwart's web interface and login pages: a data URI, a base64-encoded image or an http(s) URL, at most 1 MiB. Domains and tenants can override it in the WebUI. Clearing a value later leaves the stored setting in place; change it in the WebUI.

### System accounts

Other applications that send mail, such as notifications or password resets, need an account with a password. Once authentication runs through an external directory (`oidc.activate`), Stalwart refuses passwords an administrator sets; it only accepts app passwords it generates itself and shows once. `systemAccounts.accounts` covers this: the provisioning Job creates each account, and the reconcile Job creates an app password for it and writes it into a Secret that the applications read.

```yaml
systemAccounts:
  smtpCheck: {enabled: true, host: stalwart-headless, port: 2525}   # a listener that allows AUTH without TLS
  accounts:
    - id: mailer
      name: mailer                         # mailer@<mailDomain or domain>
      aliases: [no-reply]
      permissions: [authenticate, emailSend]   # default when omitted: send mail, nothing else; [] grants none
      appPassword:
        allowedIps: [10.0.0.0/8]           # the cluster network
      secret:
        name: mailer-smtp
        keys: [password]                   # keys that hold the password
```

The reconcile Job runs after the provisioning Job on every install and upgrade. For each account it records the app password's reference in an annotation on the Secret (`stalwart.s7n.systems/credential`):

- **Recorded credential exists in Stalwart:** nothing to do, apart from the SMTP check when `smtpCheck` is enabled, filling keys added to `keys`, and applying changed `appPassword` restrictions (`allowedIps`, `permissions`) to it in place; the password stays the same.
- **No credential recorded, gone from Stalwart, or no password in the Secret** (first installation, database reset, revocation, deleted Secret data): it creates an app password, checks it with SMTP AUTH, and writes it into the Secret. A failed check revokes it again and fails the run.

`smtpCheck` logs in like an application does. Stalwart offers AUTH without TLS only where you allow it (`MtaStageAuth`), so either point it at such an internal listener or set `startTls`.

A new app password is never created because a login failed: network errors, timeouts and failed checks fail the run, which the Job retries. App passwords marked for an account but not recorded, left by a run that died after creating one, are revoked. A lock annotation on the Secret serializes runs of different Jobs.

Applications read the password when they start. Deploy them after Stalwart, so they start with it; Stalwart needs neither the identity provider nor the applications for this, see [Server settings](#server-settings). After a new app password, for example after a database reset, restart the applications that use it; applications that store the password in their own configuration need that configuration applied again.

Secret lifecycle:

- `secret.create: true`: Helm creates an empty Secret and deletes it on uninstall. The Job writes the password.
- `secret.create: false`: pre-create the Secret. The Job updates its password keys and annotations, preserving other keys. The chart leaves it on uninstall.
- Keep one writer for the password keys and Job annotations. An external controller must not overwrite them.
- Changing `create` from true to false can delete a Helm-owned Secret. Follow an explicit ownership handover; see [reconciliation](docs/reconciliation.md).

`allowedIps` must include the Job's pod network when `smtpCheck` is enabled. Helm upgrades and `kubectl apply` are tested locally; Argo CD syncs are not.

### Webmail

Bulwark is off by default. Set a browser-accessible JMAP URL. Explicit session and administrator secrets keep admin sessions and credentials stable across pod replacements; both are required by the chart for multiple replicas:

```yaml
webmail:
  enabled: true
  jmapServerUrl: https://mail.example.org
  sessionSecret:
    existingSecret: bulwark-session   # key SESSION_SECRET, 32+ characters: openssl rand -base64 32
  adminPassword:
    existingSecret: bulwark-admin     # key ADMIN_PASSWORD
  ingress:
    enabled: true
    host: webmail.example.org
```

The browser calls JMAP on a different origin than the webmail, and Stalwart sends no CORS headers by default, so logins fail until Stalwart allows the webmail origin:

```yaml
provisioning:
  extraPlan:
    - "@type": update
      object: Http
      value:
        usePermissiveCors: false
        responseHeaders:
          Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://webmail.example.org
          Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization, Content-Type, Accept, X-Requested-With
          Access-Control-Allow-Methods: POST, GET, PATCH, PUT, DELETE, HEAD, OPTIONS
          Vary: Origin
```

Without `jmapServerUrl`, Bulwark uses the internal management Service, which only works from inside the cluster. Serve Bulwark on its own host: its base path is fixed when the image is built.

`webmail.oauth` adds Bulwark's own OAuth login, with its own client ID at the identity provider. Configure a secret for a confidential client; leave `existingSecret` and `clientSecret` empty for a public PKCE client. It is separate from Stalwart's `oidc` token validation and defaults its issuer to `oidc.issuerUrl`. Signing out also ends the session at the identity provider; set `oauth.endSession: false` when other apps share that session, `oauth.endSessionUrl` for providers without an `end_session_endpoint`, and `oauth.postLogoutRedirectUri` (registered at the provider) to return users to the login page. With `oauth.only`, `oauth.passwordFallback` keeps a password sign-in link for accounts outside single sign-on. `adminPassword` protects Bulwark's local admin dashboard, which is unrelated to Stalwart accounts.

Stalwart 1.0.3 and later give an OAuth token access to Settings → Security (passwords, app passwords, API keys) and to the admin console only with Stalwart's own scopes, which Bulwark asks for only when configured. Set `webmail.oauth.extraScopes: "urn:stalwart:oauth:scope:full"` (or `urn:stalwart:oauth:scope:security urn:stalwart:oauth:scope:settings` without admin access), then allow them for Bulwark's client: in Stalwart, in the OAuthClient's `scopes` (only a superuser can allow `full`); with an external OpenID Connect provider, create the scopes there and assign them to the client. A scope the client may not use fails the sign-in with `invalid_scope`. Bulwark also adds the configured scopes to password sign-ins with two-factor authentication.

`webmail.trustedProxyDepth` is the number of proxies in front of the pods that append `X-Forwarded-For`: 1 for an ingress controller or Gateway, 2 with a cloud load balancer in front of it. Sign-in limits and the audit log key on the client address it selects. `webmail.logFormat: json` suits log aggregation.

In local mode, Bulwark keeps state in `/app/data`. Enable `webmail.persistence.enabled` to preserve admin changes and synced settings across pod replacements. The default is a 1Gi `ReadWriteOnce` volume with the `Recreate` strategy; `webmail.strategy` overrides it. `webmail.persistence.existingClaim` mounts an existing claim instead, such as a restored snapshot or a statically provisioned volume. **On upgrades, preserve the current PVC size** (`2Gi` for the previous default): Kubernetes cannot shrink it.

Multiple replicas require `sharedStore`, `adminPassword` and `sessionSecret`. A shared filesystem cannot synchronize Bulwark's in-memory sign-in limits, leases and session revocations. Use a Redis-protocol store such as Valkey:

```yaml
webmail:
  replicaCount: 3
  sharedStore:
    existingSecret: bulwark-valkey   # key REDIS_URL: redis://:<password>@valkey:6379/0
  adminPassword:
    existingSecret: bulwark-admin    # key ADMIN_PASSWORD
  sessionSecret:
    existingSecret: bulwark-session  # key SESSION_SECRET; preserve the existing value
  settingsSync:
    enabled: true
  persistence:
    enabled: false
```

The chart does not deploy the store. It holds admin config (including OAuth client secrets), plugins, branding, sessions, synced settings and sign-in limits. Configure it with:

- persistence (for example, AOF on a persistent volume) and backups;
- `maxmemory-policy noeviction`;
- authentication and `rediss://` across untrusted networks.

Stalwart's `inMemoryStore` can share the same server if it meets these requirements. Set `sharedStore.keyPrefix` per release when sharing a store. While the store is unreachable, `/api/ready` removes pods from the Service without restarting them. If the store returns empty, restore its backup; restarting all replicas initializes fresh state. Mail remains in Stalwart.

#### Branding

`webmail.branding` sets Bulwark's logos, icons, colours and login page. Logo, icon and background settings take an https URL or a path on the webmail host. Files in `branding.files` (text, such as SVG) and `branding.binaryFiles` (base64, such as PNG) are served at `/branding/custom/<name>`; changing them restarts the webmail pods. `branding.existingConfigMap` mounts your own ConfigMap there instead; restart the pods after changing it.

```yaml
webmail:
  appName: Example Mail
  branding:
    files:
      logo.svg: |
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" ...>...</svg>
    favicon: /branding/custom/logo.svg
    themeColor: "#571efa"
    appLogo:
      light: /branding/custom/logo.svg
    login:
      logo:
        light: /branding/custom/logo.svg
        maxHeight: 96px
      layout: split
      background:
        light: https://cdn.example.org/login.jpg
        dim: 30
      companyName: Example
      imprintUrl: https://example.org/imprint
    palette:
      light: {primary: "#571efa", primary-foreground: "#ffffff"}
      dark: {primary: "#9b7bff", primary-foreground: "#0b0620"}
```

Bulwark applies a palette per hostname, so the chart sets `branding.palette` for the webmail's own hostnames: those of the HTTPRoute, else the Ingress host or `webmail.<domain>`. `branding.domains` sets [per-host branding](https://github.com/bulwarkmail/webmail/blob/main/.env.example) (`DOMAIN_BRANDING`) for other hostnames, or different names and logos per host; an exact entry for a webmail hostname replaces the palette there, and the most specific matching wildcard entry (`*.example.org`) is merged into the palette entry, so its names and logos still apply on the webmail host; its own palette colours win. A theme a user picks, or the default theme set in Bulwark's admin dashboard, overrides the palette. Settings changed in the admin dashboard (Admin > Branding) take precedence over these values.

#### Moving an existing installation onto a store

Migrate in stages so Bulwark imports the old volume before it is detached. Disabling persistence immediately also removes the chart-managed PVC unless it was previously marked to be kept. Replace the namespace and resource names below with your release's names.

1. **Back up** the webmail volume.
2. **Keep the session secret.** Bulwark encrypts stored settings with it; a different one makes them unreadable. If `webmail.sessionSecret` is already set, keep it. Otherwise copy the generated one into a Secret and reference it:

   ```sh
   kubectl --namespace stalwart exec deploy/stalwart-webmail -- cat /app/data/admin-state/.session-secret
   ```

3. **Import with one replica**, autoscaling disabled, the old volume and an empty store. Keep the PVC size and mark it for retention:

   ```yaml
   webmail:
     replicaCount: 1
     autoscaling:
       enabled: false   # otherwise replicaCount is ignored
     sharedStore:
       existingSecret: bulwark-valkey
     sessionSecret:
       existingSecret: bulwark-session
     persistence:
       enabled: true
       size: 2Gi         # use the current PVC size
       annotations:
         helm.sh/resource-policy: keep
   ```

4. **Verify the import:** the pod log lists what it copied, and `/api/ready` reports `"store":"redis"`. Files on the volume are left unchanged.
5. **Scale up** with `replicaCount`, or by re-enabling `autoscaling`, and `persistence.enabled: false`. The retained PVC stays as a backup; delete it with `kubectl delete pvc stalwart-webmail-data` once you no longer need it.

To return to one replica without a store, run one replica with the store and `SHARED_STORE_EXPORT=1` (in `webmail.extraEnv`) on a volume: it writes the store's contents back into `/app/data` and exits. Then remove `sharedStore` and the variable.

### Scaling

More than one Stalwart replica needs shared external data and blob stores (PostgreSQL and S3 with the chart's defaults) and a coordinator, which carries mailbox changes, IMAP IDLE/push and blocked-IP events between pods. The chart deploys none of these. With `replicaCount` or `autoscaling.maxReplicas` above 1, it rejects local databases and requires `coordinator.type`, or `coordinator.externallyManaged: true` when the coordinator is configured outside Helm. Stalwart refuses a coordinator while the in-memory store is its local, single-node one, so a chart-managed coordinator also requires `inMemoryStore.type: redis`:

```yaml
replicaCount: 3
podAntiAffinityPreset: soft
coordinator:
  type: nats
  nats:
    hosts: [nats.messaging.svc.cluster.local:4222]
    existingSecret: stalwart-nats   # key: password
inMemoryStore:
  type: redis
  redis:
    url: redis://valkey.cache.svc.cluster.local:6379
```

NATS is recommended for most clusters (plain pub/sub, no JetStream); `redis`, `kafka` and `zenoh` are also supported.

| Setting | Default and reason |
| --- | --- |
| `autoscaling` | Off. When enabled, the StatefulSet's replica count is left to the HPA. CPU and memory follow IMAP load only loosely; `autoscaling.metrics` accepts a connection-count metric. |
| `autoscaling.behavior` | Removes at most one pod per five minutes after ten quiet minutes, because scaling down drops that pod's IMAP and SMTP sessions. `null` restores the Kubernetes defaults. |
| `pdb` | `maxUnavailable: 1` whenever more than one replica is possible, so node drains evict one pod at a time. Single replicas get no budget and never block drains. |
| `podManagementPolicy` | `OrderedReady` starts pods one at a time. `Parallel` scales faster with an external database, but cannot be changed on an existing StatefulSet without recreating it. |
| `podAntiAffinityPreset` | `soft` or `hard` spreads pods across nodes; `topologySpreadConstraints` spreads them across zones. |

`role` and `pushShard` apply to every pod of a release; install several releases for different roles. Bulwark has its own `webmail.autoscaling` and `webmail.pdb`.

### Security

Stalwart runs as UID/GID 2000 with a read-only root filesystem, `RuntimeDefault` seccomp, and all capabilities dropped except `NET_BIND_SERVICE`. Use `extraVolumes` for additional writable paths.

Stalwart and webmail pods have no Kubernetes permissions and mount no ServiceAccount token. The reconciliation Job uses a short-lived projected token. Its Roles allow `get`/`patch` on this release's StatefulSet and `get`/`update` on the named system-account Secrets. Each Secret namespace gets its own Role. StatefulSet patch permission permits changes to the whole specification, not just the restart annotation.

The management Service is `ClusterIP`, but any pod in the cluster can reach it. `networkPolicy.enabled` restricts ingress to the Stalwart and webmail pods:

- **Mail ports** (`service.ports`) stay open to any source; `networkPolicy.mail.from` narrows them, for example to a relay network.
- **Management port**: only this release's pods (webmail, provisioning, reconcile and certificate-reload Jobs) and the peers in `networkPolicy.management.from`. Add your Ingress controller or Gateway when they use the management backend (the chart refuses to render without one), Prometheus for metrics, and in-cluster clients such as a provisioning connector.
- **Webmail port**: any source, or `networkPolicy.webmail.from`, normally the Ingress controller or Gateway.
- `networkPolicy.extraIngress` appends rules to the Stalwart policy.

```yaml
networkPolicy:
  enabled: true
  management:
    from:
      - namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx}
      - namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: monitoring}
  webmail:
    from:
      - namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx}
```

Egress is not restricted: Stalwart delivers mail to the internet and needs DNS, DNSBLs, ACME and its stores, and Bulwark its JMAP server and identity provider. The policies need a network plugin that enforces them.

[policies/kyverno/](policies/kyverno/) contains optional Kyverno `ValidatingPolicy` resources for pod hardening, digest-pinned images and resource requests. The chart does not install them. They deny by default and apply cluster-wide, so scope them before using them on a shared cluster.

## Operations

See [Upgrading](docs/upgrading.md) before changing application versions.

- **Hooks:** the provisioning Job is a Helm `post-install`/`post-upgrade` hook and an Argo CD `PostSync` hook. GitOps tools must run hooks; a plain `helm template | kubectl apply` creates it as an ordinary Job, without hook ordering, cleanup or automatic recreation on subsequent applies.
- **Secrets:** pods read environment variables at start-up. Restart them after changing an existing Secret.
- **Names:** keep the release name and `fullnameOverride` stable; volume claims are tied to them.
- **Resources:** the default requests (100m CPU, 512 MiB) suit a small installation. There is no CPU limit, to avoid throttling mail processing.

### Before production

Install and upgrade once against your real infrastructure, then check provisioning, sending and receiving mail, TLS on every port, administrator access, and restoring a backup. Test OAuth login, sessions across replicas and failover if you use them. CI cannot establish these.

## Development

Run the CI checks locally (Helm, Python and Docker required):

```sh
pip install --require-hashes -r ci/requirements.txt
scripts/render-matrix.sh            # helm lint and render ci/*.yaml and the example
scripts/validate-manifests.sh       # kubeconform and the Kyverno policies
python3 tests/render.py             # template unit tests
python3 tests/test_reconcile.py     # reconciliation unit tests (no services required)
tests/e2e/run.sh                    # reconcile Job against Stalwart and a k3s API server (Docker)
tests/e2e/kind.sh                   # the chart in a kind cluster: hooks, restart, RBAC, a consumer that logs in
yamllint -c .yamllint.yaml .
scripts/generate-docs.sh --check    # README is current
```

Once installed, `.pre-commit-config.yaml` runs Helm lint, YAML lint, documentation generation, render tests and reconciliation unit tests when relevant files change. The render matrix and manifest-policy checks must be run separately. Non-template YAML files start with `---`, end with `...` and quote every string, as enforced by yamllint.

This README is generated from `README.md.gotmpl` and the `# --` comments in `values.yaml` by [helm-docs](https://github.com/norwoodj/helm-docs): edit those and run `scripts/generate-docs.sh`. The badges read their versions from GitHub when viewed. Version and default-value changes still require regeneration of the install commands and values table.

### Release

Bitnami Common is vendored in `charts/` and updated by Renovate together with `Chart.lock`; for a manual update run `helm dependency update .` and commit both. Push a `vX.Y.Z` tag matching `version` in `Chart.yaml`: the release workflow runs CI, publishes the chart to `oci://ghcr.io/s7n-systems/charts/stalwart`, signs it with cosign and creates a GitHub release with the packaged chart and generated notes.

